dw correspondent in kiev mcconnelly  spoke earlier by phone to a resident of  gerson which is also under russian  control  this city is in southern ukraine near  the black sea coast it was seized by  russian troops in early march a week ago  russian soldiers fired on civilians who  were protesting peacefully there against  their occupiers  the 30-year-old ukrainian man who were  calling james to protect his identity  gave us his insight into everyday life  in jerusalem since the russian takeover  i've heard the explosion and  then a couple of minutes later  my friend called me up and  he told me the war is on  nothing's been delivered  neither food nor medicine so what you do  is  you you walk or you take a bicycle  for a trip in search of  money  food  and medicine  most of the coffee shops  sell now  potatoes and  cabbage instead of marshmallows and  cakes  [Applause]  at first it was  quite overwhelming and  the ukrainians expressed all the  emotions that  they felt about the invasion  by  looking into the eyes of the russian  soldiers  they kept on doing this every day for  the first  three weeks  probably you've seen on some point that  the ukrainians started uh pushing back  to  russian uh lorries  and um  on the same day  we've received a report from russia  that  some of the generals  ordered the police forces and hassan to  suppress the demonstrations he i think  i think the  ukrainian demonstration movement  crossed some lines and  it made some russian officials very  angry  we hear the explosions become  hotter and louder  and yesterday night i've seen my  hanging door vibrate  like i've never seen it  vibrate before  the ukrainians are getting closer to  town we want our army to liberate us but  we don't know what happens afterwards  and we are very much afraid of  what is happening to the city if the  ukrainian army liberates us and the  russians  remain on the left bank of nepal and  [Music]  if they start shelling at the city no  one really knows but until now they've  been shedding every city they could if  it's not in their  hands  the red cross has launched a new attempt  to get civilians out of the devastated  city of mariupol in southern ukraine the  port city has been under siege by  russian forces for weeks tens of  thousands of people are believed to be  trapped in desperate circumstances and  exposed to constant bombardment  ukrainian president vladimir zelinsky  said on friday that some people did  manage to escape against the odds  arriving to relative safety  after weeks of shelling  over 3 000 were able to escape mariopol  on friday  but it's a journey full of risks  one woman recounts her fears as she  crossed a russian checkpoint  it was very difficult she said  i was afraid they would take my son away  they made him throw his phone on the  ground and trample on it because he had  american music  an attempt by the red cross to deliver  aid and evacuate civilians  ended in failure  the organization said the lives of  thousands  depended on evacuations  uh we haven't heard anything from our  people uh in mariopol for more than one  week  there is there is still our staff  working in marijuana plus we have there  are still around six uh volunteers  working in maribel but we do not know uh  anything about their destiny if they are  live  we know that they have difficulties  because they do not have um well they  have problems with transport uh red  cross cars were partially damaged our  office was partially damaged so that's  the situation  unable to wait  others fled the bombs in cars  the white cloth a sign they mean no harm  as many as a hundred and seventy  thousand are still trapped in this city  that's been devastated by war  this former orphanage  now a makeshift bomb shelter  for those who can't escape  we came here to the basement she said  we stay here because we don't know what  happened to our apartment  we ran here with what we had  the red cross said they will attempt  once again to reach the city on saturday  in a bid to rescue the innocent  from what is left of mariopol  for the latest we can speak to our  correspondent rebecca ritters who is in  the western ukrainian city of levive  rebecca good to see you  so according to ukrainian authorities  humanitarian corridors are now in place  to evacuate people from mariupol but  what are you hearing about the today's  rescue attempts how how are they going  it's so difficult to get accurate and  up-to-date information on the  evacuations and the situation  in that city of mariupol where it's  really difficult yesterday we were sort  of getting conflicting information uh we  know that the red cross has been trying  to get into that city as you were saying  in the report there that red cross has  been trying to get into mario paul to  bring in much needed aid but to also  bring out the thousands of civilians  that are still trapped in there um we  know that those attempts so far have  failed but as you've just reported on  thursday we know that a couple of  thousands of people were able to get out  that's in a sort of a private convoy  basically people just taking a chance  taking their own private vehicles and  risking it to get out of the dire  situation of mario paul that's happening  in mario paul right now and again  yesterday another two to three thousand  also managed to evacuate we got  confirmation of that from mario paul's  city council but the red cross once  again turned around they weren't able to  get in and today i'm still i have no  confirmation on numbers or whether or  not any of those  attempts were failed or people were able  to get out but i can tell you that today  in la viv in the western part of ukraine  relatively safe compared to the rest of  the country as you can see people behind  me here out on the street out and about  the 300 or so of people from mariupol  arrived in this city so there are some  people getting out but many many more up  to a hundred and sixty thousand people  we're hearing are still trapped inside  that city of mariupol  now rebecca we're also hearing reports  about explosions in the city of venera  which we understand is near a nuclear  plant  what more can you tell us about that  that's right  well it was a series of explosions  it was confirmed by the country by  ukraine's nuclear energy agency as you  say it's near the nuclear plant of  zaparisia in in zaparisia also  significantly the town where a lot of  the people from mario poland from those  eastern  cities are actually evacuating to but uh  more concerning perhaps  is that it there it is home to the fifth  largest i believe power plant in europe  uh and it's uh sorry one of the biggest  in fact but it um gives  ukraine about a fifth of ukraine's  energy so it's a really huge plant and  of course everyone it has fears uh  bringing up memories of chernobyl 1986  that huge nuclear explosion that  happened here and nobody wants to see  anything like that that the area where  the plant is has been under russian  control since march 4th  and everyone's just uh waiting to see  what's going to happen there and very  concerned about what will happen with  the power  plant that was dw correspondent rebecca  ritters reporting from uh for us from  the city of la vie thank you so much  let's get you up to speed on some of the  other global headlines  video footage just released has revealed  to the destruction many towns of many  towns in north eastern ukraine suffered  during russia's invasion the ukrainian  state tv footage shows the town of sumi  near the russian border which has been  under siege ukrainian forces have  retaking areas in the north of the  country as russian troops withdraw  ukrainian documentary filmmaker and  photographer maxim levine has been found  dead in a village near the capital kiev  he'd been missing since mid-march  according to initial information levin  was shot by russian soldiers however  investigations are still ongoing  sri lanka has imposed a nationwide  curfew to stop protests against the  government the new restrictions will be  in place till monday morning earlier  this week demonstrators clashed with  police during protests over shortages of  basic supplies like food fuel and  medicine  hong kong authorities are asking the  entire population that's 7.4 million  people to self-test for covet next week  chief executive kerry lahm said a  compulsory universal test was essential  as the territory struggles to contain a  fifth wave of infectious operation  sergey lotznitsa is one of ukraine's  best known and most controversial film  directors for years with his features  and documentaries he has been warning  that russia was planning a war against  his country now his fiction has become  ukraine's reality  [Music]  civilians running from bombing in  ukraine  this isn't yesterday's news but a film  from 2018 set in donbass eastern ukraine  where russian forces are supporting  pro-putin separatists  the civilians are actors staging a fake  news massacre for russian tv  propaganda to justify the military  incursion  ukraine director sergey los niza shows  how for years russia planned its war of  aggression against ukraine but the west  wasn't paying attention  western liberal democracies were asleep  when the new russia was forming when it  was attacking in grozny chechnya again  when it was attacking georgia crimea  donbass and ukraine and so on  what we're witnessing now is in a way  the consequences of this sleep of this  lack of counteraction on the part of the  west  in his 2014 documentary maidan on a  pro-west uprising las nica used only  long takes to capture what he says was  the truth of the historic moment  even the style of my film is in a way  opposed to this method of propaganda  like the russian propaganda newsreels  which presented a very different picture  of what was happening in ukraine  but los niza is also controversial in  ukraine  in part because his documentary baba yar  context about a nazi massacre outside  kiev in 1941  included scenes showing locals  celebrating the arrival of the nazis  and standing by as they round up the  jews  but also because even after the russian  invasion los niza refuses to support a  full boycott of russian cinema  in response the ukrainian film academy  kicked him out  i'm absolutely against the proposed  total boycott of russian cultural  figures and russian filmmakers who've  actually been opposing the regime and  have been condemning the war because if  we do that if we boycott them as well as  everyone else in a way we'll be  betraying them  says he's no prophet and he's no  politician his films don't speak for  ukraine  but try to depict the world and the war  as he sees it  you

